  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-160968",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14128542/assets/93132c9352ed5068001c824741a1c0a7cb8dca82564ad91db706bc1ecfcedbcc.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 6,\\,\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=6$ and $\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=6$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $6$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the fourth quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{14\\pi }{9}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14128542/assets/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png)",
